One of Hawking's main research areas is quantum gravity, which is an
attempt to merge quantum mechanics and general relativity.  At least
that's what Wikipedia says

Sam Becket and Buckaroo Bonsai are both fictional, from tv's "Quantum
Leap" and "The Adventures of Buckaroo Bonzai Across the 8th Dimension" (an
odd movie that I recommend not going out of your way to see).

I got the names from looking for lists of quantum physicists and tried to
figure out who might be the most influential, important, or notable. Since
I can't remember the site, I'll rely on wikipedia

Satyen Bose: Something about Planck's law and black body radiation. Stuff
about Bose-Einstein condensates and bosons.

Enrico Fermi:  "noted for his work on beta-decay, the development of the
first nuclear reactor and for the development of quantum theory". Like
Bose, he has an elementary particle (fermion) named after him. As well as
Fermilab, which is the USDoEn's main particle accelorator or something
like that.

Lise Meitner: Experiments showed nuclear fission. She was the first to
explain the Theory of Nuclear Fission. Because she was in exile from Nazi
Germany, her former colleague (who'd asked her to figure out why his
experiments weren't resulting in fussion) published first. He got the
Nobel prize. She was eventually recognized with the Enrico Fermi Award in
1966.

Enrico, Lise, and Satyen are all also one degree of separation from one of
the other Quantum Physicists listed.

Marie Curie: Pre-quantum physics physicist who pioneered the field of
radiation. This is a little bit affirmative action, but she is one of the
more recognized (using the "my mom" test, she recognized Hawking, Curie,
Becket, and maybe another one, I don't remember).

